## Deliberate UI contract changes

- A single tap never selects an ingredient or combines. Only an owned drag/drop attempts a combination or placement. Double-tap, double-click and keyboard activation read/replay an earned explanation.
- Chapter completion does not trigger automatic progression. The player uses the explicit arrow after any earned wonder. Down means a genuinely new panel. Same-place beats and returns to known places use right; known-return accessible labels name the destination and the actual viewport still scrolls to that place.
- Objective text remains fully visible and at normal opacity throughout dragging. Only a genuinely overlapping tile can cover it, because tiles are above the HUD. Input interception is disabled during drag, not visibility.
- Ordinary reader and educational-wonder text comes first, followed by the actual italic item name. Source metadata remains in research but is not UI chrome. Physical celebrations may retain component/art presentation.
- Location-scoped short labels use actual ownership, not the panel being inspected. Maximum label variants reserve the footprint; old-save wonder replay is observational, not retrospective achievement creation.
- Weighted-third layout applies once on fresh location entry, with lower fixed hotspots and full-height cards. Restore, resize, same-place beats and revisits do not recenter existing objects.

## Preservation and evidence policy

No historical tests or witnesses are filtered out. Tests whose UI contract intentionally changed have explicit successors; exact old bytes are retained in the UX source-successor archive and original author packets. The original53 route/action/event/history baseline is replayed against the immutable original definitions, while the actual 61-world catalogue and all eight current families are tested separately. Same-ID editorial save compatibility is its own explicit proof, not an exemption from the baseline.

The lifecycle ledger adds 914 named-parent rows for the four structural editions. All historical rows remain. The 217 unpublished Waterworks rows are explicitly retained separately rather than silently deleted to match the exposed catalogue.

Focused failures, incomplete author runs, and integration fixture corrections remain in evidence. A final complete build and intact aggregate are required against the final source seal. Long quiet intervals in the aggregate are expected and are not cause to restart it.

The resolved player-text inventory is generated from final composed scenarios and current UI field selection, not a raw-source grep. It includes all label variants, reader fallbacks, objectives, entry/travel/source-update bodies, context headings, completion/skip text and wonder/caption surfaces. Independent review is AI editorial review, not a human comprehension or enjoyment trial. Logical packing and source-owned Chrome checks are not native iOS verification or a no-flash guarantee.

`tests/browser/ux-visibility.html` is a source-owned QA entry (not a Vite production entry). It runs the actual shared controller with the actual final Elements story, and records computed objective/HUD styles after genuine browser pointer events into a hidden DOM output. CUA performs the interaction and reads only that DOM evidence; no browser state injection or replacement controller is used. It does not certify native mobile events.

